---
// Article (doc 74 §5B). Merge of "Weight-loss plateaus" (2009) and "Your
// workout may be making you fat" (2008). Numbers: two months, six years,
// 500 calories, forty minutes (four).
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';

const title = 'Why Weight Loss Stalls, and Why Cardio Alone Makes It Worse';
const description =
  'Two adaptations happen at once when you diet and do the same workout: your body gets cheaper to run and better at the workout. What to change. Jamie Atlas, Denver.';
const published = '2026-09-10';
const url = `${SITE.url}/resources/why-weight-loss-stalls`;

const articleSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: title,
  description,
  url,
  datePublished: published,
  dateModified: published,
  author: { '@id': `${SITE.url}/#jamie` },
  publisher: { '@id': SITE.url },
  mainEntityOfPage: { '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': url },
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleSchema)} />

  <section class="px-6 pt-16 pb-12 md:pt-24">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <p class="font-serif italic text-[var(--color-primary)] mb-3 text-lg">
        <a href="/resources" class="no-underline">Resources</a> · Plateaus
      </p>
      <h1 class="mb-8">Why weight loss stalls, and why cardio alone makes it worse</h1>
      <p class="text-lg md:text-xl text-[var(--color-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        Two of my old posts, "Weight-loss plateaus" and "Your workout may be making you fat", were half right each.
        This is the version with the evidence in.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 py-14 bg-[var(--color-surface)] border-y border-[var(--color-border)]">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-6 text-lg text-[var(--color-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="mb-2">The plateau is not you failing</h2>
      <p>
        You cut calories, you do the same forty minutes on the same machine, it works for two months, then it stops.
        You do more of the same. It stops harder. Everyone you know has a theory and most of the theories involve you
        being lazy.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here is what is going on. Two adaptations are running at the same time, and both of them are your body doing
        its job.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mb-2 pt-4">Your body gets cheaper to run</h2>
      <p>
        Lose weight and your body lowers the cost of keeping you alive. Six years after the Biggest Loser competition,
        contestants' resting metabolism was still around 500 calories a day below what their size predicted.<sup><a href="#s1">1</a></sup>
        That's an extreme case from an extreme diet, but the direction is the same for everyone: the deficit you
        started with shrinks as you shrink.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mb-2 pt-4">Your body gets better at the workout</h2>
      <p>
        Repeat a movement and you get more efficient at it. That's what practice is. Trained runners burn less energy
        per kilometre than novices at the same speed.<sup><a href="#s2">2</a></sup> Efficiency is wonderful for a
        marathon and terrible for a plateau, because the forty minutes that cost you a lot in January cost you less in
        April, and you didn't change anything.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-text)] font-semibold text-xl">
        The same workout plus the same diet equals a smaller and smaller result. That's not a plateau. That's arithmetic.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mb-2 pt-4">Why strength training is the answer</h2>
      <p>
        Lifting changes the stimulus, so the efficiency trap resets. It also defends the tissue that burns the
        calories: muscle is what you lose when you diet without lifting, and losing it makes the first adaptation
        worse. And the numbers on the bar go up, which is the one form of progress the scale can't take away from you.
      </p>
      <p>This doesn't mean no cardio. It means cardio isn't the tool for the job you've been asking it to do.</p>

      <h2 class="mb-2 pt-4">The person this is for</h2>
      <p>
        The one who did everything the gym told them and got heavier. That person is not undisciplined. They were
        handed a plan that had an expiry date nobody mentioned.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mb-2 pt-4">What to do this week</h2>
      <p>
        Two strength sessions. Protein at every meal. Keep one cardio session if you love it, and change it every few
        weeks so your body can't settle in. And stop weighing yourself daily; see
        <a href="/resources/why-the-scale-lies">why the scale lies</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-text)] font-semibold">
        End of story. If you'd like someone to write the program around the knee or the back you've been protecting,
        <a href="/personal-trainer-denver">that's most of what I do</a>.
      </p>

      <p class="text-sm pt-4" id="s1">
        1. Fothergill E et al., "Persistent metabolic adaptation 6 years after 'The Biggest Loser' competition,"
        <i>Obesity</i>, 2016.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm" id="s2">
        2. Barnes KR, Kilding AE, "Running economy: measurement, norms, and determining factors," <i>Sports Medicine
        – Open</i>, 2015.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 py-20 bg-[var(--color-primary)] text-on-dark">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center">
      <h2 class="mb-6 text-white">Stuck at the same number? Change the stimulus.</h2>
      <p class="text-xl mb-10 opacity-95">
        A 30-minute assessment, {SITE.pricing.firstVisit}: your history, my plain read on whether I can help, and one change you can measure before you leave.
      </p>
      <a href="/book" class="cta-button cta-button-accent text-lg">Book a 30-minute assessment</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
